# A read on one connection stops every other fiber in the program.
#
# Should: the early client, which connects at 0.5s and sends at once, is
# answered at about 0.5s, while the late client's connection waits for
# its request. Chapter 06 says a fiber that blocks parks and "the scheduler
# runs whatever else is ready".
#
# Does: on every engine, the early client is answered only after the late
# one has sent its request two seconds in. net/read is a blocking read
# that holds the whole process, so the fiber serving the early client
# never runs while the late client's fiber sits in its read. Each line is
# the client's name and the second-within-the-minute it was answered:
# "early" prints within a few hundredths of "late". It also means one idle
# keep-alive client stops a whole server.
import "./srv"
import "std/net"

net/listen 0 .> (l -> net/port l .> (p -> srv/race l p))
